// Package builtin documents the PGT builtin functions, like builtin.go does for Go.
// It is never compiled or loaded: importing it with #use "builtin" is an error.
// The compiler recognizes these names itself; the signatures below are descriptive, not real code.
//
// Runtime failures (panic, out-of-range index, nil, division by zero) are implemented
// in PGT in the std/runtime package, which is linked into every program implicitly.

// fn panic(msg string)
// panic prints "panic: <msg>" to stderr, flushes stdout and terminates the program with exit code 2.
// The compiler turns the call into runtime.panicString. The argument is converted to a string.

// fn exit(code int)
// exit flushes stdout and terminates the program immediately with the given code.

// fn print(args ...)
// print writes its arguments one after another, without a trailing newline.

// fn println(args ...)
// println writes its arguments separated by spaces, followed by a newline.

// fn input(prompt string, target)
// input prints a prompt and reads a value into target (int, int64, float, char, bool, or a single-word string).

// fn inputln(prompt string, target string)
// inputln prints a prompt and reads a whole line.

// fn len(x) int
// len returns the length of a string, array or slice.

// fn append(a []T, items ...T) []T
// append returns the array with the items added; if capacity is insufficient it allocates a new block.

// fn make([]T, len int, cap int) []T
// make creates an array of zero values with length len and capacity cap (cap is optional). A negative length panics.

// fn new(T) *T
// new allocates a zero-initialized value of type T and returns a pointer to it.

// fn malloc(free[T] n) []T
// malloc allocates n elements of type T manually, for var&; implemented in std/memory (memory.alloc).

// fn free(x []T)
// free releases a block allocated by malloc; calling free again is safe (memory.release).

// fn unsafeData(a []T) *T
// unsafeData returns a pointer to the array's data.

// fn unsafeSlice(p *T, n int64) []T
// unsafeSlice wraps a block of memory as an array of length and capacity n, without reference counting.

// fn str(x) string
// str converts a value to a string.

// fn chars(s string) []char
// chars splits a string into characters.

// fn parseInt(s string) int
// fn parseInt64(s string) int64
// fn parseFloat(s string) float
// These functions parse a number from a string.

// fn substr(s string, start int, n int) string
// substr returns a substring.

// fn indexOf(s string, sub string) int
// indexOf returns the index of the first occurrence of sub in s, or -1.

// fn upper(s string) string
// fn lower(s string) string
// These functions change the case of a string.

// fn abs(x) / min(a, b) / max(a, b) / pow(x, y)
// These are simple numeric functions.

// fn rand() int
// fn srand(seed int)
// These functions implement a pseudo-random number generator.

// fn readFile(path string) string
// fn writeFile(path string, data string) bool
// fn appendFile(path string, data string) bool
// fn fileExists(path string) bool
// These functions provide basic file access.
